---
layout: base.njk
title: null
banner: true
---
{% from "icons.njk" import icon, sectionLogo %}

{# The home page is the introduction. Everything on it comes from `profile` and
   `sections` in src/_data/site.json, so adding a link or a section never means
   editing this template. #}

<div class="wrap">

  <div class="profile">

    <aside class="profile-aside">
      <img class="portrait" src="{{ site.profile.portrait | url }}"
           alt="{{ site.profile.portraitAlt }}" width="600" height="600">
      <h1 class="profile-name">{{ site.profile.name }}</h1>
      <p class="profile-role">{{ site.profile.role }}</p>
      <p class="profile-location">{{ site.profile.location }}</p>

      {# Identical geometry on every pill — same icon box, same gap, same
         padding — so they read as one set rather than five borrowed marks.
         A link with no url yet is skipped rather than rendered dead. #}
      <ul class="profile-links">
        {% for l in site.profile.links %}{% if l.url %}
        <li>
          <a href="{{ l.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            {% if l.icon %}{{ icon(l.icon) }}{% endif %}<span>{{ l.text }}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
        {% endif %}{% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <div class="profile-main">

      <section aria-labelledby="about-heading">
        <h2 id="about-heading">About</h2>
        <p>
         <p>
         Sport and animal welfare were always close to my heart and have shaped me for as long as I can remember. School years in Zambia were full of free play and where I had my first taste of team sport; school and college in India turned that into something more structured, table tennis, hockey, throwball, softball, baseball, cross-country, shot put, discus, and a good stretch of track and field events. Captaining teams, juggling packed sport and academic schedules, and having a never-say-die attitude taught me life lessons I still lean on.
         
         </p> <br>
         <p>
         I am always grateful for the various opportunities I was presented with in my 15+ years in IT corporate across India, the US and Canada. It taught me resilience and integrity, and over time built the rigour and professional standards that now run through everything I do. 6 years and counting in the non-profit sector have put that same discipline to different work, rebuilding systems that weren't working and pushing for a higher bar where it matters most.
          I am currently working at the <a href="https://takshashila.org.in/" rel="noopener">Takshashila Institution</a>,
          as Head of Policy School and Associate Dean, where I oversee all the programme management across the different courses (portfolio managemnt), write, teach and appreciate the principles of public policy.
          </p>
        </p>
        <p>
          As an educator, I teach courses that are at the intersection of sport and public policy —
          <em><a href="https://opentakshashila.net/spaces/24289612/page" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building a
          Sporting Nation</a></em>, co-taught with Nandan Kamath, and
          <em><a href="https://opentakshashila.net/spaces/21867601/page" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Transforming
          Women's Sport in India</a></em>, with Aditi Mutatkar.
          Project management runs alongside, taught to athletes, working
          professionals and undergraduates.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond the classroom: op-eds on sport, animal welfare and eldercare,
          and a monthly episode of <em>All Things Policy</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sport taught me life skills no course could imbibe! #priceless
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="work-heading">
        <h2 id="work-heading">What I work on</h2>
        <dl class="deflist">
          <dt>Programme management</dt>
          <dd>
            Twenty years and counting — from start-ups in Mysore to
            multinationals in Montreal, and from banking delivery to educational
            services. It has become a way of working rather than a job title,
            and it is what I now teach and advise on.
          </dd>

          <dt>Sports Curriculum &amp; assessment</dt>
          <dd>Learning outcomes, assessment design, graduation criteria, and the feedback cycles that improve them.</dd>

          <dt>Sport &amp; public policy</dt>
          <dd>
            Co-curate and teach two annual courses, advise the WISI Bangalore
            chapter, and mentor women moving into leadership in the sports
            sector.
          </dd>

          <dt>Writing &amp; podcasting</dt>
          <dd>
            Op-eds in the national press, podcasts, a newsletter on
            project management, and editorial support for the sports alumni
            community's own newsletter.
          </dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="made-heading">
        <h2 id="made-heading">Selected work</h2>
        <ul class="worklist">
          <li>
            <a href="{{ '/sports/timeline/' | url }}">Sporting Timeline</a>
            <span class="worklist-note">An interactive timeline of sport, politics, policy and natural events, from c. 2600 BC.</span>
          </li>
          {% for c in site.courses %}
          <li>
            <a href="{{ c.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ c.title }}</a>
            <span class="worklist-note">{{ c.note }}</span>
          </li>
          {% endfor %}
          {% for n in site.newsletters %}
          <li>
            <a href="{{ n.url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ n.title }}</a>
            <span class="worklist-note">{{ n.blurb }}</span>
          </li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </section>

      {% if site.sections | length %}
      <section aria-labelledby="sections-heading">
        <h2 id="sections-heading">Sections of this site</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-2">
          {% for s in site.sections %}
          <a class="card card-link card-section"
             href="{% if s.external %}{{ s.url }}{% else %}{{ s.url | url }}{% endif %}"
             {% if s.external %}target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}>
            {{ sectionLogo(s.logo, "") }}
            <h3>{{ s.title }}</h3>
            <p>{{ s.blurb }}</p>
            <span class="card-cta">
              {% if s.external %}Visit {{ s.title | lower }}<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span>{{ icon("external") }}
              {% else %}Open {{ s.title | lower }} &rarr;{% endif %}
            </span>
          </a>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </section>
      {% endif %}

      <section aria-labelledby="before-heading">
        <h2 id="before-heading">Before this</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Fifteen years in IT programme and delivery management at Wipro, American Red Cross, companies in the Americas and EVRY
          following ISO 9001, 27001 and CMMI Level 5 standards.
          PMP-certified. MCA from Mysore University, M.Eng from Concordia, Montreal. A special nugget I just discovered! An inter-collegiate sports festival called 
          <em>Sphygmus</em>, that I had introduced as the sports secretary at JNC in 1995-96, is running annually even to this day. 30 years and counting!
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="recognitions-heading">
        <h2 id="recognitions-heading">Recognitions</h2>
        {# Grouped by where each was earned rather than listed flat: the
           through-line is the point — school sport in Zambia, then India, then
           university, then work. #}
        <ul class="reclist">
          <li>
            <span class="rec-where">Nkana Mine Trust School &amp; Mpelembe Secondary</span>
            <span class="rec-note">Kitwe, Zambia</span>
            <ul>
              <li>First girl to play in and lead her school team at a national-level table tennis tournament in Zambia</li>
              <li>Long-distance cross-country runner</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rec-where">Bishop Cotton Girls' High School</span>
            <span class="rec-note">Bangalore, India</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Captain of school hockey team</li>
              <li>Best Outgoing Hockey Player of the Year, Grade 10</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rec-where">Jyoti Nivas College</span>
            <span class="rec-note">Bangalore, India</span>
            <ul>
              <li>Sports Secretary of the College Union</li>
              <li>Best Outgoing Athlete of the Year, final year</li>
              <li>Represented the college at table tennis, softball, baseball, cricket, hockey, throwball and athletics</li>
              <li>Captained the hockey, softball and baseball teams</li>
              <li>Represented the university and the state of Karnataka at hockey, softball and baseball</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="rec-where">Academic &amp; professional</span>
            <ul>
              <li>8th rank, MCA, University of Mysore</li>
              <li>Featured by the Avant Welfare Foundation in its
                <a href="https://awf.org.in/smb/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">She Means Business</a> series</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="contact-heading">
        {# Hidden rather than deleted: the section is still labelled by it for a
           screen reader, and the document outline stays intact. The button says
           what it does, so the word adds nothing on screen. #}
        <h2 id="contact-heading" class="visually-hidden">Contact</h2>
        {# The address is never written out as one string. The two halves sit in
           separate data attributes and are joined only when someone clicks, so
           there is nothing for a scraper to lift from the source or the text.
           Without JavaScript the button is replaced by the readable
           "user [at] domain" form, which a person can still act on. #}
        {# A real anchor, not a button running location.href. A scripted
           mailto: assignment is silently dropped when no mail client is
           registered, which looks like a dead button; a genuine link lets the
           browser and OS do their normal thing, and can be right-clicked or
           copied. The href is still assembled by script, so the joined address
           is not in the served HTML.

           The tooltip carries the obfuscated form, which is safe to render
           server-side and is also the fallback for anyone whose mail client
           never opens — they can read the address and copy it by hand. #}
        <span class="email-wrap">
          <a class="btn btn-primary email-btn" id="email-btn"
             data-u="{{ site.profile.emailUser }}" data-d="{{ site.profile.emailDomain }}"
             aria-describedby="email-tip">
            {{ icon("mail") }}<span>Email me</span>
          </a>
          <span class="email-tip" id="email-tip" role="tooltip">{{ site.profile.emailUser }} [at] {{ site.profile.emailDomain }}</span>
        </span>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</div>
